Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 16:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix CGP-4p5c-894r-qqmc: revalidate Predicated* on
 deserialization

PredicatedMap and PredicatedCollection (CWE-502) decorate a Map or
Collection to enforce configured key/value/element predicates on
every entry added through the public constructor and mutators
(put, putAll, add, addAll, checkSetValue), each of which calls the
shared validate()/checkSetValue() chokepoint. The class invariant is
that no entry or element can violate its configured predicate.

The root cause is that this invariant was never enforced on the
deserialization path. PredicatedMap.readObject() called
in.defaultReadObject() and then restored the decorated map via
in.readObject() without iterating its entries to re-invoke
validate(key, value). PredicatedCollection declared no readObject()
at all, so default deserialization restored the predicate and the
decorated collection with zero validation. Because readObject acts
as an alternate constructor that Java's serialization machinery can
drive directly from an attacker-controlled byte stream, an attacker
who supplies a crafted stream containing a PredicatedMap or
PredicatedCollection (or any of their subclasses, including
PredicatedSortedMap and PredicatedSet/Bag/List/Queue/SortedSet/
NavigableSet/SortedBag/MultiSet) whose backing store already holds a
predicate-violating entry gets back a live, application-reachable
instance that silently violates its own type contract.

The fix closes this gap by making readObject converge on the same
validate()/checkSetValue() chokepoint the constructor and mutators
already use, instead of duplicating predicate logic.
PredicatedMap.readObject() now calls map.forEach(this::validate)
after restoring the map, which re-checks both the keyPredicate and
valuePredicate slots (validate() already null-checks each predicate,
so a map configured with only one slot set does not NPE) and is
inherited unchanged by PredicatedSortedMap. A new
PredicatedCollection.readObject() iterates the restored collection
via the protected decorated() accessor (the backing field is
private) and calls validate(item) for each element, which is
inherited by all of its subclasses. In both cases an
IllegalArgumentException raised by validate()/checkSetValue()
propagates out of readObject(), so ObjectInputStream.readObject()
aborts and no partially- or fully-populated invalid instance is ever
returned to the caller.

The regression test PredicatedDeserializationExploitTest crafts
attacker-controlled serialized streams (by reflectively swapping a
validated instance's backing store for one holding a null before
serializing, since the safe constructor cannot itself produce a
violating instance) and asserts that deserializing each one either
throws the specific IllegalArgumentException or, if it must proceed,
returns nothing exposing the offending entry. It covers the
documented PoC (PredicatedMap with a null key under
NotNullPredicate), the previously-unexercised valuePredicate slot,
PredicatedSortedMap driven directly, four PredicatedCollection
subclasses, and the PredicatedCollection base class, plus positive
and preservation controls proving legitimate predicate-satisfying
instances -- including ones built with a custom Predicate
implementation and non-default Map/Collection backings -- continue
to round-trip and still enforce their predicate afterward.

